  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nREST OF TODAY/TONIGHT: Shaping up to be another gorgeous early \nOctober day with temperatures in the 70s and lower 80s. Surface high \npressure slides east today, with tightening SPG ahead of developing \ntrough across the western Dakotas and southern Canada, were seeing \ngusts 25-35 mph. Some gusts around 40 mph possible in south central \nSD. Southerly flow and WAA continues to keep conditions warmer and \ndrier, so raised highs and lowered dew points again for today as NBM \nremains too cool/moist. Warmer temperatures, dry conditions, and \nstronger winds could lead to some very locally elevated fire \nconcerns this afternoon and early evening for anywhere fuels have \nbegun to dry.\n\nWeak mid level wave moves through this evening into tonight. Breezy \nconditions continue into the overnight hours, although some gusts \naround 30 mph remain possible near/along the Buffalo Ridge with the \nLLJ increasing. May see some lower clouds develop overnight ahead of \na boundary but no precipitation is expected. Lows tonight remain \nmild, only falling into the lower and mid 50s.\n\nTUESDAY-WEDNESDAY: Northwesterly flow aloft promotes a couple of \nweak waves through the northern and central Plains; however, lack of \nmoisture keeps dry conditions in place. Surface boundary continues \nto slide east during the day Tuesday into the evening, with high \npressure moving in for Wednesday. \n\nContinued the trend of raising highs and lowering lows and dew \npoints from the NBM given the continued cool and wet bias. CONSShort \nin addition to the 90th (higher) and 10th (lower) NBM percentiles \nwere incorporated in to make those changes. Highs near and above 80 \nfor most of the area Tuesday, which is about 10-15 degrees above \nseasonal averages. Slightly cooler Wednesday thanks to more \nnorthwesterly surface flow and CAA. Highs in the 70s and lows in the \nupper 40s and lower 50s. \n\nTHURSDAY-FRIDAY: Surface high slides east Thursday, with continued \nnorthwesterly flow aloft. By Thursday night, most guidance shows a \nsurface warm front move north. Guidance is split on the track and \nstrength of any lower and mid level waves through Friday, so \nconfidence is lower during this time. In addition to timing and \ntrack differences, next biggest question is the depth of any \nmoisture return. GFS/GEFS remain more bullish on the potential for \nmeasurable precipitation, with moderate to high chances (over 40%), \nwith the ECMWF ensemble lower (40% or less). GEFS also has the \nhighest (30% or less) probability of a tenth of an inch of rain with \nother ensembles even lower. Temperatures generally near to above \nnormal. \n\nTHE WEEKEND/EARLY NEXT WEEK: Although guidance varies on the \ndetails, all deterministic models point to a shift in the pattern \nthrough next weekend and into early next week. A quick short wave \nmoves through early in the weekend. By Sunday, ridging aloft shifts \neast with troughing digging into the western CONUS, bringing \nsouthwesterly flow to the region. Low pressure strengthens and moves \neast into the early part of the week bringing more widespread \nprecipitation chances. Ensemble probabilities from the GFS and ECMWF \nshow moderate to high (over 50%) chances for at least a tenth of an \ninch of precipitation into early Tuesday.",
